    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Write each expression using algebraic conventions.\n(a) $p\\times8\\times q$\n(b) $-1\\times(u-5)$",
        "solution_text": "(a) Write the number first and leave out the multiplication signs.\n$p\\times8\\times q=8pq$\n(b) Hide the $1$, but keep the negative sign and the brackets.\n$-1\\times(u-5)=-(u-5)$"
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Write each expression using algebraic conventions. In (c), $k$ is not $0$.\n(a) $t\\times3\\times t\\times t$\n(b) $(v+7)\\div4$\n(c) $9\\div k$",
        "solution_text": "(a) Write $3$ first. The three factors of $t$ become $t^3$.\n$t\\times3\\times t\\times t=3t^3$\n(b) The whole bracket goes on top and the divisor goes underneath.\n$(v+7)\\div4=\\frac{v+7}{4}$\n(c) Keep the order of the division.\n$9\\div k=\\frac{9}{k}$"
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Show the meaning of each expression by writing all multiplication and division signs. Do not expand the brackets. In (c), $y$ is not $0$.\n(a) $-6ab^2$\n(b) $5(x+2)$\n(c) $\\frac{4}{3y}$",
        "solution_text": "(a) The power $b^2$ means two factors of $b$.\n$-6ab^2=-6\\times a\\times b\\times b$\n(b) The $5$ multiplies the whole bracket.\n$5(x+2)=5\\times(x+2)$\n(c) The whole denominator is the divisor. Keep its factors together.\n$\\frac{4}{3y}=4\\div(3\\times y)$"
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Write $r\\times-1\\times r\\times s\\div5$ using algebraic conventions.",
        "solution_text": "Move the numerical factor first, hide the $1$ and write the repeated factor as a power. Then show division as a fraction.\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}r\\times-1\\times r\\times s\\div5\\\\&=-1\\times r\\times r\\times s\\div5\\\\&=-r^2s\\div5\\\\&=-\\frac{r^2s}{5}\\end{aligned}$"
      }
    ],
